---
// A hub for "burger box packaging" (2,029 impressions) and "burger packaging
// box" (1,536), two queries the site ranked at position ~12 for with no page
// of its own -- the homepage was competing for them alongside everything else.
// One page rather than two: the queries are the same question asked twice, and
// a second near-identical page would be a doorway.
import Shell from '../layouts/Shell.astro';
import PageBody from '../components/PageBody.astro';
import { authoredPage } from '../lib/authored.js';
import GuideList from '../components/GuideList.astro';
import { PROD_ORIGIN } from '../lib/site.js';

const page = authoredPage({
  route: '/burger-box-packaging/',
  title: 'Burger Box Packaging: Types, Materials & Sizes | The Burger Boxes',
  description: 'How burger box packaging is specified: box styles, board materials, sizes, finishes and print. Wholesale prices from $0.20 a unit, minimum order 100.',
});

const schema = [{
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: PROD_ORIGIN + '/' },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Burger Box Packaging', item: PROD_ORIGIN + '/burger-box-packaging/' },
  ],
}];
---
<Shell page={page} schema={schema}>
  <PageBody postId={9001} title="Burger Box Packaging" crumb="Burger Box Packaging" route="/burger-box-packaging/">
    <p>
      Burger box packaging comes down to four decisions: the style of box, the
      board it is made from, the size it has to hold, and how it is printed.
      Get those right and the burger arrives intact and looking like it came
      from your kitchen. Get them wrong and the bun flattens, grease shows
      through, or the box costs more than it needed to. This page walks through
      each decision and links to the range that covers it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Every line here is printed to order rather than pulled from stock, so
      nothing on this page is a fixed product — it is a starting point you
      adjust. Prices start at $0.20 a unit, the minimum order is 100, and design
      and shipping are included.
    </p>

    <h2>Box styles</h2>
    <p>
      The style decides how the box opens, how it stacks behind a counter, and
      how well it holds a tall build. A clamshell hinges at the back and is the
      default for a single burger: one motion to open, one to close, and nothing
      to assemble during a rush. Parcel and takeaway boxes are squarer and
      deeper, which suits delivery runs where boxes are stacked in a bag.
    </p>
    <p>
      Window boxes put a die-cut panel in the lid so the product shows without
      the box being opened, which matters for display counters and for delivery
      photography. Foldable boxes ship flat and are assembled on site, trading a
      few seconds of labor for a large saving on storage and freight — useful if
      you are ordering in volume but short on back-of-house space.
    </p>
    <p>
      Not everything is a box. Trays hold open builds and loaded fries, liners
      sit inside a box to take the grease, inserts stop the burger sliding in
      transit, and wraps and sleeves handle the burger directly. The full range
      of styles is grouped under <a href="/product-category/boxes-by-type/">burger box styles by type</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Board and materials</h2>
    <p>
      Material governs grease resistance, how much heat the box holds, and what
      the packaging says about you before it is opened. Cardboard is the general
      purpose choice and takes print well. Corrugated board adds a fluted layer,
      which stiffens the box and insulates it — worth it for heavier builds and
      longer delivery times.
    </p>
    <p>
      Kraft is unbleached and arrives brown, which reads as plain and
      unprocessed and is why so many independents use it. Paper board is lighter
      and cheaper and suits smaller items where structure matters less. Bagasse
      is pressed from sugarcane fiber left over from sugar milling; it is sturdy,
      resists grease without a plastic lining, and composts.
    </p>
    <p>
      Recycled kraft and biodegradable stock are available where the
      environmental claim is part of the brand rather than an afterthought. The
      whole comparison, with the recycled and compostable options alongside the
      conventional ones, sits under <a href="/product-category/boxes-by-material/">burger boxes by material</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Sizes</h2>
    <p>
      Sizing is the decision people most often get wrong. A box with too much
      headroom lets the burger slide and arrive leaning; too little and the lid
      presses the bun flat and pushes the fillings out the side. The box should
      hold the build with a few millimeters to spare and no more.
    </p>
    <p>
      Standard runs cover sliders, single burgers, tall stacked builds, doubles
      and large formats, and any of them can be cut to your own internal
      dimensions instead. If you are unsure, measure your tallest regular build
      with the lid of the bun on and send that figure with the quote — pick a
      starting point from <a href="/product-category/boxes-by-size/">burger boxes by size</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Print and finish</h2>
    <p>
      Printing is where packaging does brand work rather than just carrying
      food. Full-color print is available across the range, and the coating
      changes the feel as much as the look: gloss lifts color and wipes clean,
      matte is flatter and more restrained, and spot UV puts a gloss layer over
      selected artwork so a logo catches the light against a matte background.
    </p>
    <p>
      Color-led options — black, brown, pink, red and white stock — are grouped
      under <a href="/product-category/boxes-by-color/">burger boxes by color</a>,
      and are often the quickest way to look deliberate without a full print run.
    </p>
    <p>
      Logo work, printed sleeves, branded meal boxes and stickers are collected
      under <a href="/product-category/boxes-by-customization/">custom printed burger packaging</a>,
      and can be applied to any box style on this page. Our designers prepare the
      artwork at no charge, so a logo and a rough idea are enough to start.
    </p>

    <h2>Packaging around the burger</h2>
    <p>
      Most orders do not stop at the box. Fries boxes, nugget and fried chicken
      boxes, sandwich and snack boxes, sauce pots with lids, cups for cold
      drinks and coffee, salad bowls, paper straws and delivery bags are printed
      on the same run and in the same artwork.
    </p>
    <p>
      Ordering them together is usually cheaper than ordering them separately
      and it keeps the whole handover looking like one brand rather than a
      collection of suppliers — the range is listed under
      <a href="/product-category/complementary-food-packaging/">food packaging for burger shops</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Getting a price</h2>
    <p>
      Unit price falls as quantity rises, so a firm number needs three things:
      the internal size, the style and board, and the run length. Artwork can
      follow. Send those and we come back with wholesale pricing and a
      production timeline — start on the
      <a href="/contact-us/">burger packaging quote form</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you would rather see the range first,
      <a href="/products/">every burger box and packaging line</a> is listed in one
      place. To talk a specification through instead, call (503) 358-0443.
    </p>
    <GuideList heading="Guides" intro="Longer answers to the questions that come up before a specification is settled." />
  </PageBody>
</Shell>
